{% set local_problem %}
<p>Athens' housing stock tells Limestone County's history — antebellum homes near the courthouse square, Victorian residences along tree-lined streets, post-war neighborhoods from the mid-century boom, newer developments toward the outskirts. What most share: gutter systems that are either original, inadequate, or both.</p>

<p>The older established neighborhoods near <a href="/recent-projects/">Athens State University and around downtown</a> have beautiful mature trees — oaks, maples, and pecans growing for decades. That tree coverage is Athens' charm, but it also means constant debris falling into gutters. Sectional gutters with seams every 10 feet create perfect traps for leaves, twigs, and seeds. Those seams also leak, especially after years of expansion and contraction through Alabama's temperature swings (we've recorded 70-degree swings in single days here).</p>

<p>Athens sits on Limestone County's clay-heavy soil. When gutters overflow or leak at seams, water doesn't absorb — it pools around foundations, finds cracks, and eventually causes structural damage costing thousands to repair. We've talked to Athens homeowners who didn't connect their foundation problems to failing gutters until the damage was done.</p>

<p>Seamless gutters solve these problems at the source. No mid-run seams means no leak points and fewer debris traps. Custom fabrication means gutters that actually fit your roofline — not generic 10-foot sections forced together.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If your Athens home has sectional gutters over 15 years old and you're seeing any seam leaks, replace the whole system. Patching seams is temporary — maybe 2-3 years before they fail again. The labor cost of multiple repairs approaches the cost of replacement, and you're still left with an undersized, aging system.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are exactly what they sound like: continuous gutters with no seams except at corners and downspout connections. Unlike sectional gutters made from 10-foot sections joined together, seamless gutters are fabricated on-site from one continuous roll of aluminum coil — cut to your home's exact run lengths.</p>

<p><strong>Why seams matter:</strong> Every joint in a sectional system is a potential leak point, a debris trap, and a weak spot that can separate over time. Athens' temperature swings — freezing winter nights to 95-degree summer days — stress those seams constantly. Metal expands and contracts, sealant cracks, and eventually the joints fail.</p>

<p><strong>What we install:</strong> 6-inch K-style gutters in .032 gauge aluminum. Hidden hangers at 24-inch intervals (tighter than the 32-inch code minimum). 3x4 downspouts every 35-40 linear feet. Everything measured and fabricated in your driveway to exact specifications.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">historic homes near downtown Athens</a>, we also offer half-round profiles in copper or painted aluminum that maintain period aesthetics while dramatically improving performance.</p>

<p><strong>The honest bit:</strong> "Seamless" is slightly marketing. You still have seams at inside corners, outside corners, and downspout drops. What you eliminate is the seam every 10 feet in the middle of runs — and those mid-run seams are what fail first.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've served Athens and Limestone County for over 20 years. We're not a national franchise reading from a script — we're North Alabama contractors who understand the specific challenges Athens homes face.</p>

<p>We know the difference between the older neighborhoods near <a href="/recent-projects/">Athens State</a> and the newer developments toward Tanner and Elkmont. We've worked on homes throughout the county — Victorians, ranches, colonials, and everything in between. That local knowledge matters when designing a gutter system that actually performs in Athens' conditions.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> We see a lot of "handyman special" gutter work in Athens — sectional gutters installed without proper slope, undersized for the roof area, or attached to fascia that's already rotting. Cheap installation creates expensive problems. If you're getting quotes, ask specifically about gauge thickness, hanger spacing, and whether they'll inspect your fascia before installing.</p>

<p><strong>See our work:</strong> Check out our <a href="/recent-projects/">recent projects page</a> to see gutter installations we've done across Athens neighborhoods.</p>
{% endset %}

